The Mindlin–Reissner plate theory [ 47 , 48 ] is adopted to analyze the bending behavior of rib-reinforced plates. Since rib-reinforced plates behave locally as thick plates in the rib regions, the Mindlin–Reissner theory, which accounts for transverse shear deformation, is more appropriate than the Kirchhoff plate theory, which neglects shear deformation. The displacement field is defined as follows: u ( x , y , z ) = z ⋅ θ y ( x , y ) , v ( x , y , z ) = − z ⋅ θ x ( x , y ) , w = w ( x , y ) u(x,y,z)=z\cdot	heta_{y}(x,y),\quad v(x,y,z)=-z\cdot	heta_{x}(x,y),\quad w=w(x,y) (A.1) where w w is the transverse displacement, θ x 	heta_{x} the rotation about the x x -axis, and θ y 	heta_{y} the rotation about the y y -axis. The bending strains and shear strains are given respectively as: 𝜿 = ( ∂ θ y / ∂ x − ∂ θ x / ∂ y − ∂ θ x / ∂ x + ∂ θ y / ∂ y ) , 𝜸 = ( ∂ w / ∂ x + θ y ∂ w / ∂ y − θ x )  oldsymbol{\kappa}= egin{pmatrix}\partial	heta_{y}/\partial x\ -\partial	heta_{x}/\partial y\ -\partial	heta_{x}/\partial x+\partial	heta_{y}/\partial y\end{pmatrix},\quad oldsymbol{\gamma}= egin{pmatrix}\partial w/\partial x+	heta_{y}\ \partial w/\partial y-	heta_{x}\end{pmatrix} (A.2) In the Kirchhoff theory, 𝜸 = 𝟎  oldsymbol{\gamma}=\mathbf{0} is enforced, whereas the Mindlin–Reissner theory permits 𝜸 ≠ 𝟎  oldsymbol{\gamma}
